.Im MicroEmacs command|file
.so me.tni
.TH json-format 3
.XI json-format - "Reformat the buffer as indented JSON"
.XI json-compact - "Remove all formatting from the JSON buffer"
.XI json-validate - "Check that JSON is well formed"
.XI json-context - "Toggle the sticky JSON parent context window"
.SH NAME
.Me "|json-format"
json-format \- Reformat the buffer as indented JSON
.br
.Me "|json-compact"
json-compact \- Remove all formatting from the JSON buffer
.br
.Me "|json-validate"
json-validate \- Check that JSON is well formed
.br
.Me "|json-context"
json-context \- Toggle the sticky JSON parent context window
.Me $a
.SH SYNOPSIS
.na
\fIn\fR \fBjson-format\fR [\|\fIregex\fR \fIreplace\fR\|]
.br
\fIn\fR \fBjson-compact\fR [\|\fIregex\fR \fIreplace\fR\|]
.br
\fIn\fR \fBjson-validate\fR [\|\fIregex\fR \fIreplace\fR\|]
.br
\fBjson-context\fR
.ad
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B json-format
reformats the current buffer as indented JSON, one value to a line, with the
indentation  step taken from  \fB%json-indent-width\fR  (2 by default).  Empty
objects and arrays are left compact as \fC{}\fR and \fC[]\fR.
.PP
.B json-compact
performs the reverse, removing all white space that lies outside of a string
so that the whole document is reduced to a single line.
.PP
Both commands work out which JSON to process, taking the first of the following
that applies:
.IP \fB1\fR
In the \fC*json-format*\fR buffer the whole buffer is processed again.
.IP \fB2\fR
An active region in the current buffer that, ignoring white space, starts with
\fC{\fR or \fC[\fR and ends with \fC}\fR or \fC]\fR is copied to
\fC*json-format*\fR and processed there, leaving the source unchanged. When a
numeric argument \fIn\fR is given (e.g. \fCC-u\fR), without bit \fC0x02\fR set
(see below), and the buffer can be edited
the region is replaced in place instead, with each line after the first given
the indentation of the region's first line. Checking both ends means a lone
bracket left hilighted is not taken for the region, and a region left by a yank
or a search, which hilights the pasted text or the match, is not taken as a
selection at all.
.IP \fB3\fR
A buffer in the \fCjson\fR major mode is processed in place.
.IP \fB4\fR
A buffer with no file, such as \fC*scratch*\fR, or a plain text one (a
\fC.txt\fR file or one with no specific major mode), is processed in place when its first non white character is \fC{\fR or \fC[\fR.
Buffers of other major modes (C, JavaScript etc.) are not searched for JSON,
select it as a region or copy it instead.
.IP \fB5\fR
The kill buffer, into which the system clipboard is fetched first, is yanked
into \fC*json-format*\fR and processed there when its first non white character
is \fC{\fR or \fC[\fR.
.LP
A buffer that cannot be edited, either in view mode or a pipe buffer such as
\fC*shell*\fR, is never changed; in cases 3 and 4 its content is copied to
\fC*json-format*\fR instead. The \fC*json-format*\fR buffer is shown in a popup
window and given the JSON major mode, so it can be saved, searched or viewed with
.B json-context
like any other JSON buffer. Copies are made through the kill ring and removed
from it again, without touching the system clipboard. Any narrows (folds) in the
buffer being used are removed first, as the lines they hide are part of the
document.
.PP
Both commands are string aware, matching either a complete string literal or a
single structural  character, so braces, brackets, commas and colons that occur
inside a string are never mistaken for structure. Both run
.B json-validate
first and make no change at all if the buffer is not well formed.  Both also
apply the JSON hilighting scheme to the buffer, so a scratch buffer that is not
attached to a \fC.json\fR file is coloured like one.
.PP
.B json-validate
checks that JSON is well formed without changing it. It works out which JSON to
check in the same way as
.BR json-format ,
with two differences that follow from it never making a change: a buffer that
cannot be edited is checked in place rather than copied, and any region that is
not empty is checked as it stands, whatever it starts and ends with, so a
selection with a missing closing bracket or trailing text is reported rather
than passed over for the buffer. If a problem is found the cursor is moved to
the first character of the offending token, or to the end of a region that ends
too soon, and the reason is reported on the message line. A region is checked
in a copy in \fC*json-format*\fR but the cursor is still placed in the source;
only JSON taken from the kill buffer, or pre-processed as described below, is
left in the \fC*json-format*\fR popup.
The source is left untouched, except that any narrows are removed so the hidden
lines are checked too. As the JSON
standard requires, a string may not hold a raw control character (a tab must be
written as \et) or an unknown escape, and a number may not have a leading zero.
.PP
When bit \fC0x02\fR of the numeric argument \fIn\fR is set (e.g. \fCesc 2\fR)
all three commands first make a regular expression replacement in the JSON.
This is useful for JSON held in a string literal of another language, where
every quote inside it is escaped. The user is prompted for the expression and
then for its replacement, both starting empty with the search history available;
an empty expression skips the step. For example selecting the text between the
outer quotes of the Java line
.Me $emf-scheme
.CS
String s = "{\e"name\e": \e"a\e\e\e"b\e"}";
.CE 0
.Me $ehf-scheme
and running \fCesc 2 json-validate\fR, replacing \fC\e\e\e(["\e\e]\e)\fR with
\fC\e1\fR, which removes one level of escaping so an escaped quote becomes a
quote and a doubled backslash a single one, checks
.Me $emf-scheme
.CS
{"name": "a\e"b"}
.CE 0
.Me $ehf-scheme
From a macro the expression and replacement are given as arguments, which
avoids the prompts:
.Me $emf-scheme
.CS
2 json-validate "\e\e\e\e\e\e([\e"\e\e\e\e]\e\e)" "\e\e1"
.CE 0
.Me $ehf-scheme
The replacement is only ever made in the copy in \fC*json-format*\fR, it is
made with
.Ht replace-string 2
in magic mode and the number of replacements is added to the final message. A
buffer or region is always copied, even when it could otherwise be processed or
replaced in place, so the source is never changed, and any region is taken as
it stands, as with
.BR json-validate ,
because the brackets being checked for may only appear once it has been
unescaped. As the positions in the copy no longer match the source, the result
or any error is shown in the \fC*json-format*\fR popup.
.PP
.B json-context
toggles a sticky window at the top of the frame which shows the parent lines of
the value the cursor is currently inside, in the manner of a folded outline:
.Me $emf-scheme
.CS
{
  "servers": [
    {
      "config": {
        "tls": {
.CE 0
.Me $ehf-scheme
The window is refreshed when the cursor comes to rest, and it grows and shrinks
to the number of levels being shown, up to a limit of  \fB%json-context-depth\fR
(8 by default); when the nesting is deeper than that the innermost levels are
shown.  The main window is scrolled by the same amount in the opposite direction
as the context window is resized, so the text does not appear to move.
.PP
While the context window is open \fC^G\fR closes it and then performs its usual
abort.  Issuing
.B json-context
a second time also closes it. Each buffer has its own context buffer, named
\fC*json-context:\fIname\fC*\fR, so the context of several buffers can be shown
at once.
.PP
While the context window is open the buffer's own window is locked, so pop-up
buffers such as help or the buffer list use another window. The context is
turned off and its window removed when the buffer is deleted or is no longer
shown in any window, for example when another buffer is selected into its
window, its window is deleted or
.Ht delete-other-windows 2
is used from another window.  Run
.B json-context
again to restore it.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.IP "\fC[JSON line \fIl\fC col \fIc\fC: \fIreason\fC]\fR"
The JSON is not well formed. The cursor is left on the offending token,
in \fC*json-format*\fR when the JSON was copied there (for
.B json-validate
only when it came from the kill buffer or was pre-processed), and the source is
left unchanged. The reason is one of:
.RS
.IP "\fCexpected a name or '}' but found \fIx\fR, \fCexpected a name but found \fIx\fR"
An object member must start with a quoted name; the second form follows a
\fC,\fR, after which a closing \fC}\fR is not allowed either.
.IP "\fCexpected ':' after the name but found \fIx\fR"
A name and its value must be separated by a colon.
.IP "\fCexpected a value but found \fIx\fR"
A value was required at this point.
.IP "\fCexpected ',' or a closing bracket but found \fIx\fR"
Two values follow one another with no separator between them.
.IP "\fCtrailing ',' before '}'\fR, \fCtrailing ',' before ']'\fR"
JSON does not permit a separator after the last member.
.IP "\fC'}' closes an array that was opened with '['\fR, \fC']' closes an object that was opened with '{'\fR"
The closing bracket does not match the container it closes.
.IP "\fCunexpected \fIx\fC after the end of the JSON value\fR"
Text follows the end of the document.
.IP "\fCunterminated string - no closing quote\fR"
A string was opened but never closed. A string cannot continue onto the next
line, so a raw newline in a string is also reported this way.
.IP "\fCcontrol character in a string - it must be escaped, e.g. \et\fR"
A string holds a raw tab or other control character.
.IP "\fCinvalid escape '\e\fIx\fC' in a string\fR"
Only the escapes of a quote, a backslash, a slash, \fCb\fR, \fCf\fR, \fCn\fR,
\fCr\fR, \fCt\fR and \fCu\fR are allowed.
.IP "\fC'\eu' in a string must be followed by 4 hex digits\fR"
A \fC\eu\fR escape is incomplete or not hexadecimal.
.IP "\fC'\fIx\fC' is not a valid number\fR"
An unquoted value is not a well formed number, for example \fC01\fR, which has a
leading zero.
.IP "\fC'\fIx\fC' is not a value - expected a string, number, true, false or null\fR"
An unquoted word that is not \fCtrue\fR, \fCfalse\fR or \fCnull\fR, for example
a name without quotes or \fCNaN\fR.
.IP "\fCreached the end with \fIn\fC container(s) still open - missing '\fIx\fC'\fR"
The document ends before every object and array has been closed; \fIx\fR is the
bracket that closes the innermost one.
.IP "\fCno JSON value found\fR"
The buffer or region holds nothing but white space.
.RE
.IP "\fC[JSON is valid]\fR"
.B json-validate
found no problem. When the JSON came from elsewhere the source is added,
\fC- from the region\fR or \fC- from the kill buffer\fR, followed by the
number of replacements when it was pre-processed, e.g.
\fC- from the region, 14 replaced\fR.
.IP "\fC[JSON validating... \fIn\fC%]\fR, \fC[JSON formatting... \fIn\fC%]\fR, \fC[JSON compacting... \fIn\fC%]\fR"
Shown while a large document is worked through, with \fIn\fR the percentage
done, so that the time taken by a document of many megabytes is not mistaken
for the editor having stopped. \fC[JSON is valid, formatting...]\fR (or
\fCcompacting...\fR) marks the end of the validation, and
\fC[JSON copying to *json-format*...]\fR and
\fC[JSON copying the result back...]\fR the copies made for a region, a buffer
that cannot be edited or the kill buffer.
.IP "\fC[JSON formatted]\fR, \fC[JSON compacted]\fR"
The current buffer was processed in place. When the JSON came from elsewhere the
source is added, one of \fC- from the region\fR, \fC- from the buffer\fR,
\fC- from the kill buffer\fR or \fC- region replaced\fR, followed by the number of
replacements when it was pre-processed, e.g. \fC- from the buffer, 3 replaced\fR.
.IP "\fC[No JSON found - select a region, copy some JSON or open a .json file]\fR"
None of the sources listed under DESCRIPTION applied.
.IP "\fC[JSON: too many split lines to copy back, the result is in *json-format*]\fR"
A region being replaced in place formatted to more lines over the 65520
character line limit than can be tracked; the source is left unchanged.
.IP "\fC[JSON context needs a formatted buffer - run json-format first]\fR"
.B json-context
derives the nesting from the leading  indentation of each line, so it cannot be
used on a document that is still on a single line.
.SH NOTES
These are macros defined in \fCjsonview.emf\fR.
.PP
A line in the kill buffer longer than the 65520 character line limit cannot be
yanked, so a large single line document on the clipboard is cut short and fails
to validate. Save it to a file and open that instead, lines that long are split
when a file is loaded and rejoined when it is saved. For the same reason,
copying text from such a line puts a line break where it was split, which can
fall inside a string; select the JSON as a region instead of copying it. Kill
buffer text that starts with more than 2KB of white space is not recognised. A
pre-process replacement (bit \fC0x02\fR) cannot match across the point where
such a line was split. NUL characters are dropped when a file is loaded, so a
NUL in a JSON file is never seen and cannot be reported.
.PP
.B json-context
works from the indentation rather than by parsing, which makes it cheap enough
to run as the cursor moves, but does require the buffer to have been formatted
first.
.PP
The  supporting  macro  \fBjson-path\fR  is private, but once \fCjsonview.emf\fR
has been loaded (by any of the commands above) it may be called from user macros
to obtain the position of the cursor within the document. It sets
\fB%json-path\fR to the readable  path, \fB%json-lines\fR  to the parent  lines
themselves and  \fB%json-count\fR  to the number of levels, leaving the buffer
and the cursor untouched. As a variable holds at most 2KB, \fB%json-lines\fR
keeps only the innermost \fB%json-context-depth\fR parent lines and a long
\fB%json-path\fR keeps the innermost names, starting with \fC... /\fR where the
outer ones were dropped.
